\f0\b \cf0 2. How A* Works in Simple English
\f1\b0 \
The A* algorithm is basically like a smart GPS that finds the shortest path through a maze without getting lost, and it decides which step to take next by using a simple score formula:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0            Total Score = Steps Taken + Estimated Steps Left\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Steps Taken (g):
\f1\b0  This counts the exact number of steps walked from the starting point, where every step to a neighboring block counts as 1. \
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Estimated Steps Left (h):
\f1\b0  This uses Manhattan distance, which just counts how many grid blocks away the goal is without moving diagonally, giving a fast guess. \
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Total Score (f):
\f1\b0  The algorithm always picks the grid square with the lowest total score, so it moves towards the goal fast instead of guessing randomly. \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

